viewof ts_tau = Inputs.range([0, 0.6], {value: 0.17, step: 0.01, label: "SD of speed across respondents"})
viewof ts_sig = Inputs.range([0.1, 1.2], {value: 0.5, step: 0.05, label: "Residual SD of log time (σ)"})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
ts = timeSample(Math.log(ts_med), ts_tau, ts_sig)
html`<div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:1rem">${
  Plot.plot({width: 340, height: 220, x: {label: "Seconds"}, y: {label: "Responses"},
    marks: [Plot.rectY(ts.rows.filter(d => d.t < ts.p99 * 1.5), Plot.binX({y: "count"}, {x: "t", thresholds: 40, fill: palette.light})),
            Plot.ruleX([ts.median], {stroke: palette.main, strokeWidth: 2}),
            Plot.ruleX([ts.mean], {stroke: palette.contrast, strokeWidth: 2, strokeDasharray: "4 3"})]})
}${
  Plot.plot({width: 340, height: 220, x: {label: "log seconds"}, y: {label: "Responses"},
    marks: [Plot.rectY(ts.rows, Plot.binX({y: "count"}, {x: "lt", thresholds: 40, fill: palette.main}))]})
}</div>`
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
md`Median **${ts.median.toFixed(1)} s** (solid line), mean **${ts.mean.toFixed(1)} s** (dashed); 1% of responses take longer than ${ts.p99.toFixed(0)} s.`
```

The seconds have a long tail; the log times don't. Raise σ and the mean pulls away from the median, which stays put: the gap is the tail (problem 1).

```{ojs}
