viewof bw_rho = Inputs.range([-0.9, 0.9], {value: 0.3, step: 0.05, label: "Correlation of ability and speed (ρ)"})
viewof bw_delta = Inputs.range([-2, 2], {value: 1, step: 0.1, label: "Within-person effect of taking longer (logits per SD)"})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
bw = betweenWithin(bw_rho, bw_delta)
Plot.plot({
  width: 700, height: 320, x: {label: "Log seconds"}, y: {label: "P(correct)", domain: [0, 1]},
  marks: [
    Plot.line(bw.lines, {x: "lt", y: "p", z: "id", stroke: palette.light, strokeWidth: 1.5}),
    Plot.dot(bw.people, {x: "lt", y: "p", fill: palette.main, r: 3.5})
  ]
})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
md`Across 400 simulated respondents, average log time and proportion correct correlate **${bw.rBetween.toFixed(2)}**. Within each respondent, taking one SD longer than usual changes the log odds by **${bw_delta.toFixed(1)}**.`
```

With the defaults, abler respondents are faster, so the dots slope down; yet every line slopes up, because each respondent does better when they slow down. Set ρ to −0.4 and the effect to −1 and both signs flip. The dots can't tell you the slope of the lines.

