    {text: "The pattern is between students; hurrying changes each student's own speed, a within-person question the pattern doesn't answer", correct: true, why: "Faster students may simply know more. For a given student, the speed–accuracy tradeoff predicts that hurrying *lowers* accuracy."},
    {text: "The class is too small to see a correlation", correct: false, why: "Size isn't the issue: even in a very large class, a correlation across students says nothing about what one student does when made to go faster."},
    {text: "Scores and times should be log-transformed first", correct: false, why: "A transformation changes the scale, not the level of the comparison. The pattern would still be between students."}
  ]
})
```

::: {.callout-note collapse="true"}
## Go deeper: what the pooled slope estimates under the hierarchical model

**Assumptions:** the hierarchical model with conditional independence: $\text{logit}\Pr(x_{ij} = 1) = \theta_j - b_i$ and $\log t_{ij} = \beta_i - \tau_j + \varepsilon_{ij}$, with $\varepsilon_{ij} \sim N(0, \sigma^2)$ independent of everything else; $(\theta_j, \tau_j)$ bivariate normal with correlation $\rho$ and SDs $\sigma_\theta, \sigma_\tau$; $(b_i, \beta_i)$ bivariate normal with correlation $\rho_I$ and SDs $\sigma_b, \sigma_\beta$; persons and items independent.

Condition on respondent $j$ and item $i$. Then $\log t_{ij}$ varies only through $\varepsilon_{ij}$, and $\Pr(x_{ij} = 1 \mid \varepsilon_{ij}, j, i) = \text{logistic}(\theta_j - b_i)$ doesn't involve $\varepsilon_{ij}$. So a regression of $x$ on the residual $\varepsilon$ with person and item effects has slope zero. The within-person slope is identified, and conditional independence says it is 0.

Now pool. Across all responses, log time and the logit of accuracy covary through the random effects:
$$\text{Cov}(\log t_{ij},\ \theta_j - b_i) = \text{Cov}(-\tau_j, \theta_j) + \text{Cov}(\beta_i, -b_i) = -\rho\,\sigma_\tau\sigma_\theta - \rho_I\,\sigma_\beta\sigma_b.$$
A pooled regression of accuracy on log time therefore has a slope whose sign depends on $\rho$ and $\rho_I$, even though no one's accuracy depends on their time. With $\rho > 0$ (abler respondents faster) and $\rho_I > 0$ (harder items slower), both terms are negative and slow responses look less accurate; with $\rho < 0$ the two terms pull in opposite directions. The Simulate section shows a pooled slope of −0.55 when the within-person slope is zero.

What $\rho$ is not: the effect of speed on accuracy. It describes a population of respondents. A within-person tradeoff concerns one respondent at two speeds, which experiments produce by instruction or deadline ([Heitz, 2014](https://doi.org/10.3389/fnins.2014.00150)). Neither quantity constrains the other, as the widget shows.
:::

### Does conditional independence hold? The conditional accuracy function

To see the within-person relation, keep the lognormal model's residual, $\hat\varepsilon_{ij} = \log t_{ij} - \hat\beta_i + \hat\tau_j$: how much slower (positive) or faster (negative) than expected this respondent was on this item. Plot accuracy against it, by deciles. This is a **conditional accuracy function** ([Heitz, 2014](https://doi.org/10.3389/fnins.2014.00150)). Under conditional independence it is flat. [Domingue et al. (2022)](https://doi.org/10.3102/10769986221099906) asked the question of 29 datasets from cognitive tasks and found no single shape: a respondent's marginal increases in time went with higher accuracy in some and lower in others. They also found time a weak predictor of accuracy.

#### Try it: shapes of the conditional accuracy function

Simulated data (300 respondents, 20 items) in which residual log time changes the log odds of a correct response, in the shape you choose.

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
viewof cf_shape = Inputs.radio(["none", "rising", "falling", "inverted U"], {value: "inverted U", label: "Shape"})
viewof cf_s = Inputs.range([0, 1.5], {value: 0.5, step: 0.05, label: "Strength (logits)"})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
cw = cafWorld(cf_shape, cf_s)
Plot.plot({
  width: 700, height: 280, x: {label: "Decile of residual log time (1 = much faster than expected)", ticks: 10}, y: {label: "Proportion correct", domain: [0.2, 0.8]},
  marks: [Plot.ruleY([cw.overall], {stroke: palette.guide, strokeDasharray: "4 3"}),
          Plot.line(cw.deciles, {x: "decile", y: "p", stroke: palette.main, strokeWidth: 2.5}),
          Plot.dot(cw.deciles, {x: "decile", y: "p", fill: palette.main, r: 4})]
})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
md`A straight-line logistic regression of the response on residual log time gives a slope of **${cw.slope.toFixed(2)}**.`
```

With "none", the curve wobbles around the dashed line: the sampling noise of 600 responses per decile. With "inverted U", responses near the expected time do best, and the linear slope is close to zero. A single slope summarises a curve that only rises or only falls; it can report no relation when there plainly is one.

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
quiz({
  q: "The widget's inverted U gives a linear slope near 0. Does conditional independence hold in those data?",
  options: [
    {text: "No: accuracy depends on residual time, just not linearly", correct: true, why: "Conditional independence says the curve is flat everywhere. A curve that rises and falls violates it, and a straight line through it averages the two halves to about zero."},
    {text: "Yes: the slope is zero", correct: false, why: "A flat curve gives a zero *linear* slope, but so does a symmetric one. Look at the deciles, not only at the slope."},
    {text: "We can't tell without the person parameters", correct: false, why: "The residual already removes each respondent's speed and each item's time intensity; the pattern across deciles is the evidence."}
  ]
})
```

### Is speed constant?

Checking the first assumption needs the order in which items were given. [Domingue, Kanopka, Stenhaug, Soland, Kuhfeld, Wise and Piech (2021)](https://doi.org/10.1111/jedm.12291) had it for an adaptive reading test and found **response acceleration**: a response ten positions later took about 88% as long, and one thirty positions later about $0.88^3 \approx 68\%$ as long. Respondents with lower ability estimates accelerated more, and for them faster responses were less accurate than expected. Very fast responses at chance are the extreme case, **rapid guesses** ([Wise & Kong, 2005](https://doi.org/10.1207/s15324818ame1802_2)).

#### Try it: response acceleration

A respondent speeds up over 40 items of equal time intensity.

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
viewof ac_ratio = Inputs.range([0.7, 1], {value: 0.88, step: 0.01, label: "Time ratio per 10 positions"})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
ac = acceleration(ac_ratio)
Plot.plot({
  width: 700, height: 260, x: {label: "Position in the test"}, y: {label: "Seconds", domain: [0, 65]},
  marks: [Plot.ruleY([ac.level], {stroke: palette.guide, strokeDasharray: "4 3"}),
          Plot.line(ac.rows, {x: "position", y: "t", stroke: palette.main, strokeWidth: 2.5})]
})
```

```{ojs}
//| echo: false
md`A response 30 positions later takes **${(100 * ac.after30).toFixed(0)}%** as long. A model with one speed fits the dashed line (${ac.level.toFixed(1)} s), so its residual log time runs from **${ac.first.toFixed(2)}** at the first item to **${ac.last.toFixed(2)}** at the last.`
```

With one speed per respondent, the change lands in the residuals: early responses look slow, late ones fast. If accuracy also changes over the test, the conditional accuracy function picks that up, and the two assumptions fail together.

::: {.callout-tip appearance="simple"}
**If you've done** [Guessing and priors](guessing-priors.qmd): in `roar_lexical`, 3.1% of responses came in under 0.3 seconds, at an accuracy of 0.51 on a task where chance is 0.5, and a handful of respondents gave most of them.
:::

## Simulate

The code generates 200 respondents × 20 items from the hierarchical model with $\rho = 0.4$ and conditional independence (`delta <- 0`), and fits log time with `lmer()` and accuracy with `glmer()`. The first run takes 30 to 45 seconds, most of it loading `lme4`.

With the seed as written, the estimated abilities and speeds correlate 0.38 (truth 0.40). The pooled slope of accuracy on log time is −0.55, yet the within-person slope is −0.09 (SE 0.07) and the deciles are flat: the pooled slope is all between people and items. Set `delta <- -1` and the within-person slope comes back at −1.01, with deciles falling from 0.71 to 0.36. Set `rho <- -0.4` with `delta <- 0`: the pooled slope shrinks to −0.16, as able-and-slow respondents offset hard-and-slow items, and the within-person slope stays near zero.


::: {.cell}
```{webr}
#| results: markup
#| warning: false
#| message: false
#| include: true
#| label: unnamed-chunk-19
# Simulate responses and response times from the hierarchical model: a Rasch model
# for accuracy, the lognormal model for time, correlated person parameters (theta,
# speed) and correlated item parameters (difficulty, time intensity). Then fit the
# two halves with lme4 and ask what the data say about speed and accuracy.
#
# delta is a within-person speed-accuracy tradeoff: the change in the log odds of a
# correct response when a response takes one unit of log time longer than expected.
# The hierarchical model has delta = 0 (conditional independence).
#
# nAGQ = 0 makes glmer() skip its slowest step, so the fits take seconds in the
# browser. In your own R session, delete ", nAGQ = 0" and raise np and ni.
library(lme4)
set.seed(53)
np <- 200      # respondents
ni <- 20       # items
rho <- 0.4     # correlation of theta and speed across respondents
delta <- 0     # within-person effect of taking longer than expected (log odds)

# Person parameters: theta (SD 1) and speed tau (SD 0.3), correlated rho.
z1 <- rnorm(np); z2 <- rnorm(np)
theta <- z1
tau <- 0.3 * (rho * z1 + sqrt(1 - rho^2) * z2)
# Item parameters: difficulty b and time intensity beta (log seconds), correlated 0.5.
w1 <- rnorm(ni); w2 <- rnorm(ni)
b <- w1
beta <- log(30) + 0.3 * (0.5 * w1 + sqrt(1 - 0.5^2) * w2)

d <- expand.grid(id = factor(1:np), item = factor(1:ni))
e <- rnorm(nrow(d), 0, 0.5)                          # residual log time
d$lrt <- beta[d$item] - tau[d$id] + e
d$resp <- rbinom(nrow(d), 1, plogis(theta[d$id] - b[d$item] + delta * e))

m_time <- lmer(lrt ~ 1 + (1 | id) + (1 | item), d)
m_acc <- glmer(resp ~ 1 + (1 | id) + (1 | item), d, binomial, nAGQ = 0)
tau_hat <- -ranef(m_time)$id[, 1]
theta_hat <- ranef(m_acc)$id[, 1]
cat(sprintf("Correlation of theta and speed: true %.2f, estimates %.2f\n",
            rho, cor(theta_hat, tau_hat)))
cat(sprintf("Correlation of b and time intensity: in these items %.2f, estimates %.2f\n",
            cor(b, beta), cor(-ranef(m_acc)$item[, 1], ranef(m_time)$item[, 1])))

# Pooled: accuracy on raw log time, ignoring who and what.
pooled <- glm(resp ~ lrt, binomial, d)
# Within: accuracy on residual log time, with person and item effects.
d$res <- residuals(m_time)
within <- glmer(resp ~ res + (1 | id) + (1 | item), d, binomial, nAGQ = 0)
cat(sprintf("Pooled slope on log time: %.2f\n", coef(pooled)["lrt"]))
cat(sprintf("Within-person slope on residual log time: %.2f (SE %.2f); true delta %.2f\n",
            fixef(within)["res"], sqrt(vcov(within)[2, 2]), delta))
d$decile <- cut(d$res, quantile(d$res, 0:10 / 10), include.lowest = TRUE, labels = FALSE)
cat("Proportion correct by decile of residual log time:\n")
print(round(tapply(d$resp, d$decile, mean), 2))

```
